Numerous generalisations are possible, for example:
\begin{itemize}
\item Relax the requirement of independent coordinates in Theorem~\ref{cube} to that of weakly dependent vector-valued variables;
\item Instead of equidistributions, consider distributions with strongly log-concave probability  densities;
\item Use various simple and robust nonlinear classifiers like small neural cascades (compare to Theorem~\ref{CascadeSep}), algebraic classifiers and other families. For these generalisations, the VC dimension is expected to play the  role similar to  dimension $n$ in Theorems \ref{ball1point} and \ref{cube}.
\end{itemize}
